<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo noupdate="0">
    <!--
        Real field-level access control for purchase-cost / profit fields
        (stock.lot cost/profit fields, purchase.order.line.price_unit,
        product standard_price) lives in Python, NOT here - see
        models/field_security.py.

        An earlier version of this module tried the commonly-documented
        <record model="ir.model.fields"><field name="groups" .../></record>
        XML pattern, which is what Odoo core/OCA modules used in older
        versions. On this Odoo 17 instance, ir.model.fields.write() (see
        odoo/addons/base/models/ir_model.py) unconditionally refuses to
        alter ANY property of a field whose state != 'manual' (i.e. any
        field defined in Python by a module, not created via the UI) -
        "Properties of base fields cannot be altered in this manner! Please
        modify them through Python code, preferably through a custom
        addon!" - confirmed live when installing this module the first
        time. The actually-supported mechanism is exactly what that error
        message says: redeclare the field on an inheriting model in Python
        with the `groups` keyword - Odoo's field-merging across the MRO
        keeps every other property from the original definition. Kept as a
        comment here (rather than silently dropped) so nobody re-adds the
        XML version and re-triggers the same install failure.

        The ir.rule gap-fix below is unaffected by any of this - ir.rule is
        a completely different mechanism (row-level, not field-level) and
        writes to it are not restricted the way ir.model.fields is.
    -->

    <!--
        Defense-in-depth, not (as an earlier version of this comment
        claimed) closing an active leak: aquarium_fish_management's own
        ir.model.access.csv grants aquarium.fish.mortality and
        aquarium.fish.transfer read+write+create to base.group_user - i.e. to
        EVERY internal user, regardless of role, because that module was
        built before this role matrix existed. Checked live via
        `res.groups.read` on point_of_sale.group_pos_user's `implied_ids` on
        this instance: it is EMPTY - Cashier does NOT in fact inherit
        base.group_user through it (confirmed by reading a real Cashier test
        user's own `groups_id`: only Aquarium Cashier + Point of Sale/User,
        nothing else). So today, Cashier is already blocked from
        aquarium.fish.mortality/transfer by ir.model.access.csv alone, before
        this rule is even evaluated - the rule below is a deliberate
        safety net, not a fix for a live leak, in case a real Cashier
        account ever also ends up with base.group_user for some other
        legitimate reason (portal features, a future Odoo version changing
        that implication, etc.) - directly violating "Cashier: no fish
        management" is the failure mode this guards against, not one
        observed in the field.

        ir.rule can restrict access beyond what ir.model.access.csv grants (the
        two combine with AND, unlike multiple ir.model.access.csv rows for the
        same model which OR together) without editing that module's own
        security files. Scoping domain_force to `[('id', '=', 0)]` (never
        matches - id 0 is never a real record; Odoo 17's newer domain
        validator rejects the classic `[(1, '=', 1)]`/`[(1, '=', 0)]`
        int-literal trick used in older Odoo versions, confirmed live via
        "Invalid domain: 'int' object has no attribute 'split'" on install)
        under a rule whose `groups` is set to ONLY group_aquarium_cashier
        means it applies exclusively to Cashier-role users -
        Fish Staff/Manager/Admin are unaffected since they don't carry that
        group.
    -->
    <record id="rule_aquarium_cashier_no_fish_mortality" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="name">Aquarium Cashier: no fish mortality access</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="aquarium_fish_management.model_aquarium_fish_mortality"/>
        <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('group_aquarium_cashier'))]"/>
        <field name="domain_force">[('id', '=', 0)]</field>
        <field name="perm_read">True</field>
        <field name="perm_write">True</field>
        <field name="perm_create">True</field>
        <field name="perm_unlink">True</field>
    </record>
    <record id="rule_aquarium_cashier_no_fish_transfer" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="name">Aquarium Cashier: no fish transfer access</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="aquarium_fish_management.model_aquarium_fish_transfer"/>
        <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('group_aquarium_cashier'))]"/>
        <field name="domain_force">[('id', '=', 0)]</field>
        <field name="perm_read">True</field>
        <field name="perm_write">True</field>
        <field name="perm_create">True</field>
        <field name="perm_unlink">True</field>
    </record>
</odoo>
